@Ashley-QwQ newer burn is much better, idk why. Maybe the quadratic thing broke something since I just used raw calibration data this time. Or disc is closer to the calibration one in its dimensions. Btw since 1 ppm difference makes such a difference, would thermostat help? Maybe it;s temperature difference playing a big role since material expands and shrinks due to temperature differences. 

At the edge where the text is it still curves significantly enough to see it... But that is actually ok since text appears cursive lol.

How exactly do you calibrate? Just fitting 22th degree polynomial or linear approximation between points? Notice that I added the range parameter to cover the outer edge. Is it just precision loss due to CAV mode? Can it be calibrated in CLV mode like the recent bluray PR does? Maybe more points would help? I understand that more points means more time for calibration and more wear on the drive since it needs to make more reading head moves to complete. Maybe, if you distribute points evenly, make additional more dense points inside last 5-6 mm of outer space near the outer edge of the disc?

@Ashley-QwQ A correction: burn is better but it also curves at the inner side. Not that noticeable but I have found a contrast line and let me draw over a previous burn how it looks (exaggerated a bit but still)
