@Ashley-QwQ Okay, I will try with range from like 100 to max number of sectors lol. And with 80 rungs. I have a flatbed scanner, how exactly do I need to use the scan calibration tool? I understand I need test grid and burn it with DiscImageStudio and then scan it, but how exactly I need to call `gridscan` and `correction`? If I have e.g. 80 rungs will those tools take it into account? Do those tools provide corrected DiscImageStudio preset or the internal `geo` data for discprobe? I ask because I want to see how far the angular resolution can actually be pushed. You said difference in one batch is around 0.2°. That's tolerable for usual pictures but for photolithography masks repeatability it's not enough. Maybe the only way to achieve better precision ia to calibrate on each disc separately with a custom hardware modified drive. 

Thanks for the multithreading. I will set a reminder for `git pull` tomorrow (for me already today), although I would only have 2 hours at most, starting from 22:00 (now it's November 10th, 00:29 in Moscow). I hope to test the flatbed scanner calibration to achieve the best quality for lisa disc (best angular precision and 23200х23200 picture!). 

Btw on our job we use highest quality PCBs with 0.1mm track width and spacing for LVDS lines that connect ADCs and FPGA. That's very small batches. We don't have any equipment to make our own PCBs but I will track your progress with photomasks and show my test burn that showcased the 5 um precision to some engineers at our lab. Maybe there would be some useful application for the technology. We in Russia have some stupid bureaucracy regarding documents that forces us to use CDs to send some files such as source code to different organizations. The not so stupid part is backing up all documents, text and drawings, on DVDs (because you know one big solar flare and all data in flash memory is gone). Thus we have plenty of DVDs, CDs and drives. I hope the whole image burning gets some traction since of course we also have flatbed scanners for calibration.

@Ashley-QwQ thanks for the scroll images and explanation. I will try it this evening with 80 rungs. That's actually good because I need to physically move scanner near my laptop to connect it with usb cable, otherwise I can only do jpg/pdf relatively low quality scans with email based scan sharing. That time the 80 rungs will be done on my Verbatim random data disc. I finally got permanent markers yesterday and properly marked all the discs I have so far.

Regarding Chinese — I will consider the fact that you are native speaker. We have a good school here so we have native speakers talk with us 45 minutes once a week (main teacher is still Russian native speaker). Also once a week I have online 2-hour long HSK4 preparation course with very experienced and respected professor, she doesn't know Russian well so the entire lesson is in Chinese, although she of course carefully chooses words we already know to speak. When I was in Beijing I met two guys in restaurant and managed to talk with them half through the translation app, half without it. The complained about Chinese majnland having many dialects lol. So that's a proof Chinese themselves consider it a problem. We of course study 普通话 so we have many stories from Russian teacher abput horrors of working as a translator and encountering somebody from Hebei province or Hong Kong.

The wobble idea seems elegant enough and it's sad that disc drives can't measure the empty sectors timing. The compromise is maybe to burn roughly one full revolution of random data per rund and then measure it? For usual images it won't be noticeable since it's tens of 0.74 um random rings across the entire disc, but it may actually provide a per-disc calibration without one disc wasted. Or just use the random data disc ring for tge image where quality isn't that important since maybe the rings will show up. Tell me please if such method is possible for calibration, especially on bluray since it uses CLV and its track spacing is more than twice as tight.

@LTVA1 It's less bad than three points, luckily (◍•ᴗ•◍) Each open zone can start with a band of random data a few millimetres wide, and a band like that holds thousands of turns, so `geo` can put many rungs inside each one, as many as you like. What three zones limit is where the measured parts are, not how many points you get: inside the bands everything is measured, and the picture between them is interpolated from the fit.

And your Verbatim discs are DVD+R, which is the better case here: DVD+R allows up to 16 open fragments instead of three, so the bands could be spread much more evenly across the disc. All of this is still a plan though, we haven't written the burning side for it yet.

On the 22 rungs: from your logs, what hurt on the Verbatim discs looked more like noisy reads than too few points, since the four runs on one disc only agreed within 0.3 to 1.3°. Your 80-rung run tonight will show it nicely: if 80 rungs land on the same curve as the good 22-rung runs, the points weren't the problem. And `geo` now reads a few check points again at the end and tells you if the drive changed during the run, so a noisy run should say so itself.

@Ashley-QwQ the ring I meant is probably a lead-in. Maybe it's less noticeable on DiscImageStudio burned discs.

I will try the test pattern iso generation with one worker a bit later.

You wrote the corrections measures only up to the last grid line, so would a second correction actually help innermost and outermost areas? You say it probably won't so there is no way to fix those edges?

@LTVA1 We may have found the cause of the half-radius change, at least on our setup. We burned the same picture ISO twice on the same batch of RITEK DVD-R in our GH70N:

- at the drive's lowest speed (4x): 26 of 96 sampled blocks failed to read back, all of them from about a third of the way through the disc (about 39 mm) outwards; the inner part read perfectly;
- at 8x: all 96 read back, and a dense scan from 37 to 46 mm found no failures at all.

So on our drive the slowest speed is the worst one for these texture images, and the change starts at a fixed point in the burn, not on the disc. Random data (like the measuring disc) is not affected, which is why timing still works out there.

If you can spare a disc, it would be great to know whether the same holds on your drive: burn one of your pictures at a different speed from the one that gave the paler band, and see if the band (or the contrast step on your Ritek discs) goes away. Which speed did the banded discs use?

@LTVA1 Glad that's found! A spindle with both kinds is a sneaky one (´∀｀) For the DVD-R ones:

- Run `geo` on one of them, then burn a grid with that new result and scan it. The two corrections you have belong to the DVD+R geometry, so start the DVD-R corrections fresh, without `--base`.
- Speed: your DVD-R failed partway at 8x, so try 4x on the next one. If `info` on the failed disc shows where the write stopped, I'd still like to see it.
